{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "Under the Labor Code, wages must be paid directly to the worker (Art. 105) except when force majeure or a special circumstance—determined by the Secretary of Labor and Employment—justifies payment through another person with the worker’s written authority; the payment then satisfies the employer’s liability. Moreover, Art. 116 makes it unlawful for any person to withhold any portion of a worker’s wages or to induce the worker to give up wages by force, stealth, intimidation, threat, or any other means without the worker’s consent. These provisions apply regardless of the worker’s location, so any retention of wages that is not authorized by the worker or justified by a force‑majeure circumstance is prohibited.",
